To investigate the structure and turbulence in a protoplanetary disk, we utilize a simulated global disk model across various disk layers and Reynolds numbers. Our simulation encompasses both solid boundaries and a global scope, spanning from the mid-plane to the upper layers, and we have developed an innovative method for generating turbulence in upper disk levels. To achieve this, we apply a novel approach that utilizes azimutal-symmetric flow sources, bypassing the finite bean-shaped flow domain restriction. With our technique, we can generate turbulence in upper disk levels and compare it with observations, especially in regions beyond the sub-stellar regime. Our simulation allows for a comprehensive study of disk structures and turbulence across various disk layers and Reynolds numbers, and our results indicate that turbulence behaves differently in the mid-plane and upper levels, aligning with theoretical predictions. Additionally, we found that azimutal-symmetric modes dominate in upper disk levels and our results match observational data in several aspects, including surface brightness, disk gap widths, and turbulence levels. With turbulence levels measured across the disk, we found that they increase linearly with Reynolds numbers, validating a hypothesis first proposed by Cuzzi et al. (1979). With this approach, we can generate realistic particle distributions that are comparable to observations, especially in terms of scattering indices and azimuthal velocity structures. With turbulence levels measured across the disk, we found that azimuthal velocity structures are proportional to $\sqrt{
